React version: 19.3.0
Chromium version: 153.0.8010.12

Using the file system API (with [`showSaveFilePicker`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/showSaveFilePicker) which is currently only avaliable in Chrome) with an async transition results in `isPending` remaining `true` for much longer than expected, well after the initial promise has resolved.

Testing in the code reproduction has an average after five runs of 17.592 seconds after the promise has resolved for `isPending` to become `false`.

Removing the `startTransition`s with setting state directly results in an instant update as expected.

This is a snippet of how the transition code is written.

```jsx
startTransition(async () => {
  try {
    const newHandle = await showSaveFilePicker();
    const writableStream = await newHandle.createWritable();
    await writableStream.write(newBlob);
    await writableStream.close();
  } catch (e) {
    if (e instanceof DOMException && e.name === "AbortError") {
    } else {
      throw e;
    }
  }
  startTransition(() => {
    setBlob(newBlob);
  });
});
```

## Steps To Reproduce

1. Use `useTransition` with `isPending` and `startTransition`
1. Use `startTransition` with an async function that uses the File System API to write a file
3. Use `startTransition` with a state setter
4. Wait for `isPending` to become true

Link to code example:

https://github.com/jeremy-code/react-save-file

## The current behavior

`isPending` remains true even well after the promise has resolved, on average 17 seconds, when the async File System APIs are used with `startTransition`.

## The expected behavior

The state should be updated and `isPending` should become false shortly after the promise has resolved, since there is no other rendering/work being done.

